WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.833
Narrator: Europe is leading

00:00:00.833 --> 00:00:02.953
one of the most important space

00:00:02.953 --> 00:00:05.577
travel efforts of this century.

00:00:05.577 --> 00:00:07.825
It's a mission called RemoveDEBRIS,

00:00:07.825 --> 00:00:10.116
and if all goes according to plan,

00:00:10.116 --> 00:00:12.864
it could save the future of space travel

00:00:12.864 --> 00:00:14.215
as we know it.

00:00:18.801 --> 00:00:22.051
The objective of
RemoveDEBRIS is just that.

00:00:22.051 --> 00:00:23.775
Demonstrate how we could remove

00:00:23.775 --> 00:00:25.525
space debris that's cluttering up

00:00:25.525 --> 00:00:26.805
Earth's orbit,

00:00:26.805 --> 00:00:28.728
including pieces of used up rockets

00:00:28.728 --> 00:00:31.019
and retired satellites.

00:00:31.019 --> 00:00:32.947
Even though you can't
see it from the ground,

00:00:32.947 --> 00:00:35.219
there are more than half a million

00:00:35.219 --> 00:00:37.787
chunks of space trash flying around

00:00:37.787 --> 00:00:39.017
our planet,

00:00:39.017 --> 00:00:40.435
and most of it is concentrated

00:00:40.435 --> 00:00:42.117
in low earth orbit,

00:00:42.117 --> 00:00:44.967
where it whips around at 28,000 kilometers

00:00:44.967 --> 00:00:46.234
an hour.

00:00:46.234 --> 00:00:48.753
Fast enough to fly from LA to London,

00:00:48.753 --> 00:00:51.008
in just 19 minutes,

00:00:51.008 --> 00:00:52.688
but here's the trouble with that.

00:00:52.688 --> 00:00:53.869
Low earth orbit is our

00:00:53.869 --> 00:00:55.810
front door to the universe.

00:00:55.810 --> 00:00:57.290
To get anywhere in space,

00:00:57.290 --> 00:00:59.724
whether it's the
International Space Station,

00:00:59.724 --> 00:01:01.631
Mars, or beyond,

00:01:01.631 --> 00:01:04.319
we have to cross that threshold first,

00:01:04.319 --> 00:01:06.069
but if you have a bunch of garbage

00:01:06.069 --> 00:01:07.178
blocking your path,

00:01:07.178 --> 00:01:09.109
it's gonna be difficult.

00:01:09.109 --> 00:01:09.942
Even worse,

00:01:09.942 --> 00:01:12.867
experts, like astrophysicist
Donald Kessler,

00:01:12.867 --> 00:01:14.751
fear that just one collision

00:01:14.751 --> 00:01:16.669
between two large objects,

00:01:16.669 --> 00:01:19.853
could seal up that opening forever,

00:01:19.853 --> 00:01:21.249
because it would set off

00:01:21.249 --> 00:01:22.878
a chain reaction.

00:01:22.878 --> 00:01:24.793
One collision after another,

00:01:24.793 --> 00:01:26.145
after another,

00:01:26.145 --> 00:01:28.318
which could produce
thousands of additional

00:01:28.318 --> 00:01:29.976
pieces of debris.

00:01:29.976 --> 00:01:31.986
This domino effect is called the

00:01:31.986 --> 00:01:33.166
"Kessler Syndrome,"

00:01:33.166 --> 00:01:35.372
after Donald Kessler himself,

00:01:35.372 --> 00:01:37.192
and it predicts that low earth orbit

00:01:37.192 --> 00:01:39.696
could become littered with space trash,

00:01:39.696 --> 00:01:41.686
making it too dangerous for spacecraft

00:01:41.686 --> 00:01:42.827
to fly through,

00:01:42.827 --> 00:01:44.646
or also reduce their life spans

00:01:44.646 --> 00:01:46.424
because of collisions.

00:01:46.424 --> 00:01:47.257
That's where Europe's

00:01:47.257 --> 00:01:49.677
RemoveDEBRIS mission comes in.

00:01:49.677 --> 00:01:50.816
In 2018,

00:01:50.816 --> 00:01:52.776
it successfully tested the first

00:01:52.776 --> 00:01:54.806
of four stages of its trash

00:01:54.806 --> 00:01:56.105
clean up technology,

00:01:56.105 --> 00:01:58.576
and it looked like this.

00:01:58.576 --> 00:02:00.408
This is not an illustration.

00:02:00.408 --> 00:02:03.849
This is actually happening in space.

00:02:03.849 --> 00:02:05.067
That's a net about the size of

00:02:05.067 --> 00:02:07.038
a large dining room tablecloth,

00:02:07.038 --> 00:02:09.716
ensnaring a small spacecraft

00:02:09.716 --> 00:02:11.158
that the mission team planted

00:02:11.158 --> 00:02:12.948
for the experiment.

00:02:12.948 --> 00:02:14.616
Now, this was just the first

00:02:14.616 --> 00:02:15.478
of what will, hopefully,

00:02:15.478 --> 00:02:17.391
be many more tests,

00:02:17.391 --> 00:02:18.549
because in the future,

00:02:18.549 --> 00:02:20.577
we're gonna need a bigger net.

00:02:20.577 --> 00:02:22.077
Guglielmo S. Aglietti:
At the end of the day,

00:02:22.077 --> 00:02:24.449
the final goal is to be able to

00:02:24.449 --> 00:02:26.706
capture very large targets,

00:02:26.706 --> 00:02:28.668
so, possibly what one of the targets

00:02:28.668 --> 00:02:30.919
that has been mentioned,
as a potential one,

00:02:30.919 --> 00:02:33.111
is an old satellite,

00:02:33.111 --> 00:02:34.671
Envisat,

00:02:34.671 --> 00:02:36.837
and it is maybe the size of a bus.

00:02:36.837 --> 00:02:38.383
Narrator: Scientists
have been proposing ways

00:02:38.383 --> 00:02:39.508
to clean up space junk

00:02:39.508 --> 00:02:41.264
since the early 90s.

00:02:41.264 --> 00:02:42.331
They've thought of everything,

00:02:42.331 --> 00:02:43.256
from lasers,

00:02:43.256 --> 00:02:44.828
to robotic claws.

00:02:44.828 --> 00:02:46.729
But this is the first time any concept

00:02:46.729 --> 00:02:49.375
has actually been tested in space.

00:02:49.375 --> 00:02:50.671
The team hopes to test its other

00:02:50.671 --> 00:02:52.357
three technologies, as well,

00:02:52.357 --> 00:02:53.740
which involve a harpoon,

00:02:53.740 --> 00:02:55.783
for spearing the trash,

00:02:55.783 --> 00:02:58.103
a navigation system for
analyzing the trash's

00:02:58.103 --> 00:02:59.938
size, distance,

00:02:59.938 --> 00:03:00.873
and rotation,

00:03:00.873 --> 00:03:03.168
and a sail for slowing the trash down,

00:03:03.168 --> 00:03:04.001
so it can reenter,

00:03:04.001 --> 00:03:06.412
and burn up in Earth's atmosphere.

00:03:06.412 --> 00:03:08.814
But all this technology isn't the reason

00:03:08.814 --> 00:03:10.803
this mission has come closer than any

00:03:10.803 --> 00:03:11.652
before it.

00:03:11.652 --> 00:03:14.842
Aglietti: Everybody agrees,
that to remove some of

00:03:14.842 --> 00:03:15.825
this debris,

00:03:15.825 --> 00:03:17.799
is the right thing to do,

00:03:17.799 --> 00:03:19.363
but, clearly, the issue is going to be,

00:03:19.363 --> 00:03:20.245
you know, the cost.

00:03:20.245 --> 00:03:21.734
Narrator: The RemoveDEBRIS project has

00:03:21.734 --> 00:03:23.909
cost 15 million euro,

00:03:23.909 --> 00:03:26.087
or, about 17 million US dollars.

00:03:26.087 --> 00:03:27.578
Aglietti: The current
mission was sponsored

00:03:27.578 --> 00:03:29.620
by the European community,

00:03:29.620 --> 00:03:30.661
and, again, we had, you know,

00:03:30.661 --> 00:03:32.871
ten partners, working on this project,

00:03:32.871 --> 00:03:35.033
and, overall, the cost of this project

00:03:35.033 --> 00:03:36.057
was about, you know,

00:03:36.057 --> 00:03:37.791
€15 [million].

00:03:37.791 --> 00:03:40.064
When you are going to do

00:03:40.064 --> 00:03:41.164
this kind of mission,

00:03:41.164 --> 00:03:42.022
for real,

00:03:42.022 --> 00:03:44.029
the cost will be higher,

00:03:44.029 --> 00:03:46.786
and, so, I think the various stakeholders

00:03:46.786 --> 00:03:47.911
around the world,

00:03:47.911 --> 00:03:49.860
have to get together,

00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:52.515
and decide how such mission could

00:03:52.515 --> 00:03:53.353
be funded.

00:03:53.353 --> 00:03:54.604
We are talking about not just,

00:03:54.604 --> 00:03:55.537
you know, the UK,

00:03:55.537 --> 00:03:57.281
or, maybe, NASA, but also,

00:03:57.281 --> 00:03:59.147
China, you know, Russia, and so on.

00:03:59.147 --> 00:04:00.606
The problem is, again,

00:04:00.606 --> 00:04:01.659
who is going to pay for it?

00:04:01.659 --> 00:04:04.195
Narrator: So, Europe has taken the lead.

00:04:04.195 --> 00:04:05.841
Rest of the world,

00:04:05.841 --> 00:04:07.424
it's your turn now.

